# Production Roleplay Requirements

## Purpose

This document consolidates the most important production requirements for the roleplay-focused app. The app should be safe, consistent, editable, and robust before it is considered production-ready.

## Core design principles

1. Structured turns are canonical.
2. Characters, lore, and memories are separate domains.
3. Users must be able to modify, branch, and roll back scenes.
4. Memory is evidence-based and reversible.
5. The app should be local-first and private by default.
6. Streaming is required for good usability.
7. The app must degrade gracefully when structured generation fails.

---

## 1. Structured streaming

The frontend must stream partial generation results as they arrive.

### Required event flow

```text
generation_started
turn_started
turn_delta
narrator_delta
turn_completed
cast_update
memory_update
generation_completed
```

### Rules

- Show the first character turn as soon as a valid speaker is known.
- Render narrative prose immediately after the relevant speaker turn.
- Never mix partial narration into a partially rendered dialogue bubble.
- Keep event ordering stable even if model output arrives out of order.
- Allow the user to stop generation at any time.
- If generation is cancelled, discard incomplete partial turns and preserve only valid committed turns.

### UI behavior

- A `Generating...` indicator is shown for the current response.
- The user sees the narration appear immediately after the speaker turn.
- Partial responses remain clearly marked as interim until the final turn sequence is validated.

---

## 2. Turn editing and regeneration rules

Editing an earlier message must invalidate later derived state.

### Required behavior

When the user edits a message:

1. Mark later turns, memories, and summaries as stale.
2. Recompute downstream context from the edited point.
3. Reevaluate speaker attribution and cast updates.
4. Rebuild all summaries that depend on the edited segment.
5. Preserve historical versions as a branch or checkpoint.
6. Show the user whether a later branch was created or a regeneration was triggered.

### Developer requirement

The system must track a `turn_revision_id` for each frame of the story. Regenerating a later segment should not silently overwrite earlier verified text.

---

## 3. Branching and rollback

Roleplay systems are iterative by nature. Branching is essential.

### Required features

- Save a checkpoint at the current state.
- Restore a previous checkpoint.
- Branch from any turn.
- Keep the main branch and side branches separately.
- Compare branch summaries.
- Offer restore and merge operations only when they are safe.
- Archive abandoned branches instead of deleting them without a warning.

### Branch data

A branch snapshot should include:

- conversation state
- turn sequence
- cast state
- lorebook entries used
- summary version
- active memories
- open threads
- world state
- unrelated settings

---

## 4. Memory conflict resolution

Memory should be evidence-driven, reversible, and manageable.

### Required rules

When a newer fact conflicts with an older fact:

- keep the evidence trail for both sides
- mark the older fact as superseded or stale
- prefer the newer fact only if it is validated or user-confirmed
- preserve the earlier fact for search and audit
- allow the user to override the system's choice

### Example

```text
Old memory: Jade lives in the eastern district.
New memory: Jade moved to the western district.
```

The app should not silently delete the old fact. It should create a status update:

```text
Superseded by newer evidence from message 147.
```

### Memory statuses

- active
- stale
- superseded
- rejected
- user-confirmed
- provisional
- merged

---

## 5. Narrator semantics and scene state

Narrator turns should not always be implied to follow a character. Some narration describes the world, environment, pacing, time, or consequences without a single speaker.

### Required attributes

```json
{
  "type": "narrator",
  "text": "Thunder rolls beyond the windows.",
  "related_speaker_ids": [],
  "scene_effect": true,
  "follows_turn_id": "turn-42"
}
```

### Rules

- Narrator turns can be scene-level and disconnected from a specific speaker.
- Character dialogue should never be rendered inside narrator style.
- A narrator turn may follow a character turn, but it should not always do so.
- Name tags and scene cues must remain separate from the dialogue bubble.

---

## 6. Prompt and content safety separation

Imported characters, lorebooks, and prior conversation text may include instructions. They are **not** system instructions.

### Required separation

```text
SYSTEM RULES
CHARACTER DEFINITIONS
LOREBOOK ENTRIES
MEMORY FACTS
SCENE STATE
CURRENT USER INPUT
```

### Rules

- App rules always have highest priority.
- Prompt-embedded instructions from imported content never override safety policies.
- Character cards and lorebooks remain user-authored story data.
- The system must validate and redact untrusted content before insertion.

---

## 7. Conversation locking and concurrency

Only one generation job should be active per conversation by default.

### Required behavior

- One response generation per conversation at a time
- Ability to stop generation
- Request IDs and generation tokens
- Safe cancellation cleanup
- Retry logic with backoff for transient provider issues
- Clear UI warning when a prior generation is still in progress

### Concurrency risks

- multiple background memory jobs overwriting each other
- duplicate speaker attribution runs
- branch updates racing each other
- two generations writing the same conversation state

The backend must serialize writes by conversation ID.

---

## 8. Native model-provider abstraction

The app cannot hard-code a single model stack.

### Minimum provider capabilities

- structured output
- streaming
- context-length awareness
- stop generation
- health check
- model metadata
- fallback on provider failure

### Supported providers may include

- Ollama
- llama.cpp server
- LM Studio
- OpenAI-compatible API
- Azure OpenAI compatibility
- embedded local reasoning model plus remote host model

### Default configuration

Default local flow:

- local reasoning model for planning and memory extraction if enabled
- local chat model for final roleplay output
- SQLite as the default storage layer
- no required external vector database

---

## 9. Backup and migration system

The app must support migration-safe saves.

### Data included in a backup

- conversations
- turn log
- cast entries
- memory entries
- summaries
- world state
- lorebooks
- character cards
- settings without secrets
- skill versions
- checkpoint metadata

### Requirements

- schema version included in every backup
- backup before migration
- migration warnings shown to the user
- partial restore supported when safe
- import validation before writing

---

## 10. Privacy controls

The app should be private by default.

### Required settings

- Local-only mode
- Clear current conversation
- Clear all memories
- Export selected memories only
- Disable memory extraction
- Disable automatic summaries
- Disable telemetry by default
- Exclude secrets from export
- Password-protected or encrypted local storage optional

---

## 11. Memory quality dashboard

The app should be able to measure whether memory works.

### Metrics to include

- memory recall rate
- wrong-memory rate
- summary compression ratio
- prompt token savings
- character consistency score
- relationship consistency score
- lore activation precision
- speaker attribution accuracy
- latency per response

These should be visible in a debug/benchmark panel, not hidden in logs.

---

## 12. Recommended final architecture

```text
User input
  ↓
Context builder
  ├─ active cast
  ├─ relevant lore
  ├─ memory retrieval
  ├─ world state
  ├─ summary
  ├─ current scene
  └─ active skill(s)
  ↓
Response planner
  ├─ speaker selection
  ├─ narration triggers
  ├─ max-speaker policy
  └─ structured turn schema
  ↓
Model provider
  ↓
Turn normalizer
  ↓
Database write + memory extraction + summary update
  ↓
UI render
  ├─ UserTurn
  ├─ CharacterTurn
  ├─ NarratorTurn
  └─ turn grouping
```

---

## Final delivery standard

The app is production-ready only when it can do all of the following without corrupting story continuity:

- stream multi-speaker roleplay responses correctly
- preserve separate user, character, and narrator turns
- allow editing and rollback safely
- resolve memory conflicts without data loss
- maintain local-only privacy defaults
- export and restore complete sessions safely
- handle concurrency and cancellation correctly
- show the user what memory was retrieved and why

This is the bar the project should meet before being described as robust.
